Current Company- Big Fintech Europe
YOE- 2+

**Question**-

Implement a class which has 2 functions-
Fun 1. Insert the Interval L-R
Fun 2. Given a number check if that number is present

Example-

(2-5)
(9-13)
Check for 10, output- Yes
Check for 6, output -No

Follow ups and the clarifying questions which I asked in the interview-
1. Are all the numbers L-R positive integers? --- Yes
2. What max value of L-R are we looking at?-- Initially it was 10^6 lets assume but later he said it can be 2^32.
3. In the L-R is the L<R or can it be R<L as well?-- He said it can be either way
4. How is the input provided? Is the L-R passed to fun1 as separate parameters like
    op1-> fun1(int L,int R)
	op2-> or in a array like fun1( Array[Pair<L,R>]) ?
	-- He didnt tell me how to assume the input to be. I implemented it using op1, had it been op2 the problem might have been very simple because we are given the queries beforehand and we are not checking calling fun2 in between the insertion of intervals. 
5. Can we have overlapping intervals like 2-5, 7-13, 9-13?--- Yes


**My answer**-

sol1 -

Make a boolean array of size 10^6. Assume the indices of array as the intervals. Iterate array over L-R and mark the indices as TRUE. For checking if a number is present simply return array[Number].
pseudocode-**

		vector<int> ranges;
		fun1(int L,int R)
		{
			for(int i=L;i<=R;++i) ranges[i]=true;
		}
		fun2(int number)
		{
			return ranges[number];
		}
In this case
TC- fun1- O(N) fun2- O(1). 
SC- O(10^6).

Follow up is like lets assume we can have number up to 2^32 how will u proceed in that case since we cant store it in array now due to memory constraints. We dont care about time now but memory should be handled.

sol2- 

Lets create a map<int,int>. Insert L-R like map[L]=1, map[R]=-1. fun1 Insert will take O(LogN) time. Now for fun2 I will Iterate over all key,value in my map and maintain a counter to check if I am in valid range or not and if I find my number i will return true else false.
pseudocode-

		map<int,int> ranges;
		fun1(Int L,int R)
		{
			ranges[L]=1;
			ranges[R]=-1;
		}
		fun2(int number)
		{
			int inRange=0;
			for(range:ranges)
			{
				if(number<range.first and inRange==1) return true;
				inRange+=range.second;
			}
			return false;
		}

In this case 
TC- fun1- O(logN) fun2- O(NlogN). 
SC- O(total no of times fun1 L-R is called).

Further thoughts- These type of questions can be done using a Segment Tree/ Interval Tree but implementing that in a 45min interview is not that easy.

**RESULT**-

I got rejection mail the same day. How could I improve my solution. What can be the reason for rejection? Does the interviewer want me to-
1. implement it in a specific way.
2. Maybe I overthinked the solution trying to optimise the TC.

Please comment what are your thoughts on this and areas where I can improve?
